WEBVTT

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:05.175
Robots act in the real world. Every control decision matters.

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:11.654
The Unexpired Plan turns a previously computed plan into a free monitor.

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:19.825
Accelerated diffusion policies save computation by taking shortcuts.

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:24.932
But when a shortcut changes the action, can the robot still finish?

00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:33.850
First, remove the monitor. A deliberate bias shifts the accelerator's
proposal.

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:38.936
The same reference schedule remains, but the block misses its target.

00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:45.526
Now replay the same scene with the Unexpired Plan monitor.

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:52.957
The saved reference rejects the drift. A conservative candidate completes
the placement.

00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:58.266
Same start. Same disturbance. Same playback speed.

00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:05.937
This controlled illustration shows the mechanism. It is not a measured
benchmark success rate.

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:12.092
A reference predicts a chunk, but only its first two actions execute.

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:19.138
Later calls compare their next actions with the same indices of that saved
plan.

00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:25.502
The comparison needs no new forward pass. When the plan expires, the
reference runs again.

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:31.138
The deviation check detects a proposal that drifts away from the plan.

00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:36.359
But simply repeating the old plan can make that deviation vanish.

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:44.390
A second check detects near repetition in recent emitted chunks. Both checks
can veto.

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:52.297
After a veto, execute the saved reference slice and step down to a more
conservative accelerator.

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:59.164
That demotion is never undone within the episode. The response matters as
much as the signal.

00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:04.943
In physical work, a small action change can matter.

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:12.511
Shared hardware motivates the problem. Our reported evaluations use
simulation.

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:18.980
Score each candidate on the states its own actions visit, using the ninety-
fifth percentile.

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:24.756
Select the cheapest candidate that passes. A low mean can hide a damaging
tail.

00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:32.576
Four policy families show substantial compute savings, with every control
call guarded.

00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:39.579
Push T remains undecided. Its confidence interval is too wide to establish
equivalence.

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:44.792
On three families, the reference schedule alone is already within the
margin.

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:54.082
The stronger test is a fifth family: tight-tolerance insertion, after every
design choice was frozen.

00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:59.262
Without the monitor, both comparisons fall outside the two-point margin.

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:06.045
The monitored arm is inside the margin, at nearly twice the effective speed.

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:14.981
Effective speedup includes reference computation. Full coverage has a
compute ceiling.

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:20.641
Refreshing too late leaves accelerated calls without an unexpired reference.

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:25.886
Compute savings do not imply faster tasks or guaranteed safety.

00:03:27.700 --> 00:03:31.687
An unexpired reference. A veto that is never forgotten.

00:03:33.700 --> 00:03:38.356
Accelerate the policy, keep a meaningful reference, and preserve the task.

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:47.693
With thanks to the researchers who shared their footage, and the open source
community.
